UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 6, Lesson 1: Running, jumping and landing', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Running, jumping and landing are fundamental movement skills. Good running has bent arms swinging opposite to the legs, eyes forward and landing on the balls of the feet. For a safe jump, swing the arms, push off with both feet and land softly with bent knees, feet about shoulder-width apart and arms forward for balance. Warm up first, choose a clear, flat space and stop if anything hurts.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nMei jumps over a line, lands with straight legs and falls backwards. Write two cues that would fix her landing and say why they protect her body.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCues: ''bend your knees'' and ''land with feet apart and arms forward''. Bent knees absorb the force like springs, and wide feet and forward arms keep balance. Stiff, straight legs send the impact into the knees and back.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking the farthest jump is the best jump. A strong jump has good technique and a balanced landing; distance improves with practice.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nSet up a safe, clear space with a start line. Practise ten standing jumps and a short run using your own cue words. Use a recording sheet to rate your technique (arms, knees, landing) from 1 to 3 and set one goal. Skip jumping if you have any pain.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Running, jumping and landing are fundamental movement skills. Good running has bent arms swinging opposite to the legs, eyes forward and landing on the balls of the feet. For a safe jump, swing the arms, push off with both feet and land softly with bent knees, feet about shoulder-width apart and arms forward for balance. Warm up first, choose a clear, flat space and stop if anything hurts.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-MSP-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Set up a safe, clear space with a start line. Practise ten standing jumps and a short run using your own cue words. Use a recording sheet to rate your technique (arms, knees, landing) from 1 to 3 and set one goal. Skip j Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using running technique on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking the farthest jump is the best jump.. Correction: Cues: ''bend your knees'' and ''land with feet apart and arms forward''.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking the farthest jump is the best jump. A strong jump has good technique and a balanced landing; distance improves with practice. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating running technique on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with running technique, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about running technique with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for running technique. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for running technique. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Cues: ''bend your knees'' and ''land with feet apart and arms forward''","Thinking the farthest jump is the best jump","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson"],"question":"Mei jumps over a line, lands with straight legs and falls backwards. Write two cues that would fix her landing and say why they protect her body. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for running technique; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Running, jumping and landing are fundamental movement skills","Cues: ''bend your knees'' and ''land with feet apart and arms forward''","Careful use of jumping and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Thinking the farthest jump is the best jump"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with running technique?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named jumping and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name jumping and explain why it matters only, without linking it to running technique","Apply running technique to new material and name the jumping and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Thinking the farthest jump is the best jump"],"question":"Which response best applies running technique with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on running technique. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a put-down and call it honesty","Thinking the farthest jump is the best jump","Cues: ''bend your knees'' and ''land with feet apart and arms forward''","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for running technique?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Thinking the farthest jump is the best jump","Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with running technique"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about running technique?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Running, jumping and landing are fundamental movement skills.) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a put-down and call it honesty","Example cues. RUN: ''bend arms, pump arms, look ahead''. JUMP: ''knees bend, arms swing, push''. LAND: ''soft knees, feet","Thinking the farthest jump is the best jump","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about running technique?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for running technique?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name jumping and explain why it matters only, with no linked example","Set up a safe, clear space with a start line","Retype the worked example with no new example","Thinking the farthest jump is the best jump"],"question":"Which independent response best applies running technique to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about running technique with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating running technique on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using running technique on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for running technique. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with running technique, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking the farthest jump is the best jump.. Correction: Cues: ''bend your knees'' and ''land with feet apart and arms forward''.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w06-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise running technique.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Movement Skill and Performance","success_criteria":["I can use running technique with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify jumping and explain why it matters.","I can practise a soft landing independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a safe space needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Running, jumping and landing are fundamental movement skills.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Running, jumping and landing are fundamental movement skills","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about running technique with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample cues. RUN: ''bend arms, pump arms, look ahead''. JUMP: ''knees bend, arms swing, push''. LAND: ''soft knees, feet apart, arms forward, hold still for two seconds''. Sasha jumps a chalk line, measures 90 cm and lands steadily with bent knees.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMei jumps over a line, lands with straight legs and falls backwards. Write two cues that would fix her landing and say why they protect her body.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCues: ''bend your knees'' and ''land with feet apart and arms forward''. Bent knees absorb the force like springs, and wide feet and forward arms keep balance. Stiff, straight legs send the impact into the knees and back.","explain":"Running, jumping and landing are fundamental movement skills. Good running has bent arms swinging opposite to the legs, eyes forward and landing on the balls of the feet. For a safe jump, swing the arms, push off with both feet and land softly with bent knees, feet about shoulder-width apart and arms forward for balance. Warm up first, choose a clear, flat space and stop if anything hurts.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking the farthest jump is the best jump. A strong jump has good technique and a balanced landing; distance improves with practice. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Running and landing cues.","caption":"Cues keep you balanced.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Run</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">bent arms, eyes ahead</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Land</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">soft knees, arms forward</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example cues. RUN: ''bend arms, pump arms, look ahead''. JUMP: ''knees bend, arms swing, push''. LAND: ''soft knees, feet apa Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example cues. RUN: ''bend arms, pump arms, look ahead''. JUMP: ''knees bend, arms swing, push''. LAND: ''soft knees, feet apa Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about running technique with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample cues. RUN: ''bend arms, pump arms, look ahead''. JUMP: ''knees bend, arms swing, push''. LAND: ''soft knees, feet apart, arms forward, hold still for two seconds''. Sasha jumps a chalk line, measures 90 cm and lands steadily with bent knees. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Soft and stiff landings.","caption":"Bend the knees.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Soft</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">bent knees absorb force</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Stiff</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">straight legs jar the body</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about running technique with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking the farthest jump is the best jump. A strong jump has good technique and a balanced landing; distance improves with practice. Here is a more helpful example: Example cues. RUN: ''bend arms, pump arms, look ahead''. JUMP: ''knees bend, arms swing, push''. LAND: ''soft knees, feet apart, arms forward, hold still for two seconds''. Sasha jumps a chalk line, measures 90 cm and lands steadily with bent knees.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCues: ''bend your knees'' and ''land with feet apart and arms forward''. Bent knees absorb the force like springs, and wide feet and forward arms keep balance. Stiff, straight legs send the impact into the knees and back.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking the farthest jump is the best jump. A strong jump has good technique and a balanced landing; distance improves with practice. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about running technique with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRunning, jumping and landing are fundamental movement skills. Good running has bent arms swinging opposite to the legs, eyes forward and landing on the balls of the feet. For a safe jump, swing the arms, push off with both feet and land softly with bent knees, feet about shoulder-width apart and arms forward for balance. Warm up first, choose a clear, flat space and stop if anything hurts. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 6, Lesson 1: Running, jumping and landing","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Fundamental movement skills","week":6,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample cues. RUN: ''bend arms, pump arms, look ahead''. JUMP: ''knees bend, arms swing, push''. LAND: ''soft knees, feet apart, arms forward, hold still for two seconds''. Sasha jumps a chalk line, measures 90 cm and lands steadily with bent knees.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Running and landing cues.","caption":"Cues keep you balanced.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Run</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">bent arms, eyes ahead</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Land</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">soft knees, arms forward</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Soft and stiff landings.","caption":"Bend the knees.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Soft</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">bent knees absorb force</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Stiff</text><text x=\"358\" y